# Literature

The evidence base — one note per paper, with the takeaways that made it worth
keeping and, where it applies, the reason it is no longer.

A note's status is about the paper's **standing in this anthology**, not about
whether the paper is correct. That distinction is the point of keeping notes
and [practices](../practices/README.md) apart: a foundational paper can carry
advice that is now wrong, and a paper retired as uninteresting can still be
the source of something everybody does. Under the old schema both facts lived
in one field on the paper, so neither could be said.

`Rejected` is the attic. It is a real place in the record with real entries,
and a practice still citing one of them is a finding — `luria lint` reports it
rather than leaving it to be noticed.

Roughly a third of these notes produced no practice at all. They are here
because they are worth reading.

A note's own topic will not tell you which of those it is. **The two schemes'
topic vocabularies overlap; they do not nest.** A note can be tagged
`adaptation-and-tuning` or `inference-optimization` — words absent from the
practice vocabulary — and still carry a recommendation the record files
happily under `training-optimization`, which is where its fine-tuning,
preference-training and quantization practices already live.

The seven practice topics range over **how you train**, not over what you are
training. So the question a note has to answer is whether the work contains an
instruction expressible in those seven — never what field it came from. A
diffusion paper whose contribution is a noise-level sampling distribution and
a loss weighting is a `training-optimization` paper; a segmentation paper
whose contribution is a model-in-the-loop annotation bootstrap is a
`data-pipeline` paper. Reading a note's tag, or its domain, as a scope
boundary is how a paper stays unfiled for a year.

